# Performance — nrscholar-frontend

Users are children on low- and mid-range Android phones, often inside the WebView.

- **Lazy-load** heavy screens and libraries (`three`, `@react-three/*`, `@tsparticles/*`, `lottie-react`, `react-pdf`). `App.tsx` already lazy-loads battles, missions and the reader, so keep new heavy screens lazy too.
- **Clean up** on unmount: dispose three.js scenes/geometries/textures, stop particles and Lottie, clear `setInterval`/`setTimeout`/`requestAnimationFrame`, and remove listeners. Leaks crash low-end WebViews.
- **Animations:** prefer transform/opacity. Limit simultaneous particles and looping Lottie files. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- **Network:** don't refetch `/api/users/me` per component (`api.ts` caches it). Run independent requests in parallel. Avoid request waterfalls and requests per list item.
- **Re-renders:** avoid huge single-component screens where every state change re-renders everything. Split into sub-components with local state. Memoize only measured hot spots.
- **Assets:** compress images and Lottie JSON, size images explicitly, use `loading="lazy"` for off-screen images. Don't import large JSON/content bundles into the main chunk.
- **Mobile (RN):** `FlatList` for long lists, memoized list items, no heavy work on the JS thread during animations.
- Measure (React Profiler, Network tab, Chrome remote debugging of the WebView) before and after, and report the numbers.
